On Monday, February 3, 2003, at 01:08  PM, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:
> I do have a question... who owns the rights to the EV8?  I know the 
> chip
> will probably never see silicon, but how far along was it in 
> development?
> Wishful thinking I'm sure, but might an interested party build it?

My understanding is that EV8 and all Alpha technology (lock, stock and 
barrel) was sold to Intel. (Under EV8, Alpha technology was reserved by 
Compaq, but ?licensed? to Intel -- whatever the lawerish words that 
allow Samsung to do whatever they can do under their pre-existing 
contract which covers something -- I don't know if Samsung can produce 
EV7 or only EV6.)

As for EV8 seeing silicon... the most recent rumors I've heard are that 
Itanium 2005 will truly be "Alpha Inside." I doubt that means that EPIC 
is gone, but apparently there have been some pretty massive 
architectural changes to deal with the fact that existing IA64 designs 
not only ran slow but very HOT. IA64-2005/6 will be a very different 
processor than IA64-2002/3.

As part of the Alpha deal, Intel also received the Digital Compiler 
technology -- generally considered the best in the industry. Many of 
the design issues with IA64 were "work-arounds" for the fact that Intel 
and HP did NOT have sufficient compiler technology to do optimization 
in the compile phase; they were forced to do the extra work in silicon. 
(I've also heard rumors that performance gains in IA64 to date have 
been largely as a result of Intel's deploying of the Digital Compiler 
technology last year; not silicon improvements.)

Personally, I would not be surprised to discover IA64-2005 support a 
binary compatibility mode much like the old VAX supported PDP and Alpha 
supported VAX. It might just be an emulator, but there should be enough 
horsepower that it could be done as an aid to migration. Digital and 
IBM made massive strides in emulation technology a number of years 
back. IBM's work allowed Apple to support 68K processor code on 
PowerPC. Digital allowed "Binary Emulation Mode" on the Alphas. [Don't 
forget the IA64 design can change Endian-ness - I think on a per 
instruction basis; and it has contained all of the lock-step 
instructions since the first design ?5+ years ago before Compaq bought 
Tandem.]

It's not rocket science any more... however, it is all marketing 
decisions.

I've been getting the sense that while Alpha itself is dead, and we are 
giving up several years of architecture leadership, the engineering IS 
surviving.

Since 1999 it has been widely held that only the Alpha and Power 
architectures contained a future. PARC aznd x86 capitulated early. 
SPARC doesn't know it's dead yet. Power4 received a substantial boost 
in R&D in the years since. And Intel bought the Alpha intellectual 
property. Don't forget IA64 is neither fish nor fowl -- it is a new 
design that is still very much a work in progress. The jury is still 
out on Hammer and Transmeta - neither may be in the same game (HPC).

T.T.F.N.
William H. Magill
