On Mon, 3 Feb 2003, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:

> Also, I don't think I ever got an answer to building the entire redhat
> system from scratch via srpm.  Is there an easy way of doing this?  Mike @
> Redhat said this would be of great use to them, but is there a script or
> something similar to where if I have all the SRPMs in a chroot (I would need
> help setting that up too...) and I can launch it and have it build all night
> (or days...)?

There is not an easy answer to this one on auto-rebuilders.  
I ahve a trivial script which walks through SRPMS, building 
what is can, and leaving error file remnants of what it cannot 
at:
   ftp://ftp.owlriver.com/pub/local/ORC/ORCrebuild/ORCrebuild

and tried to kick off a discussion on the topic at on 
rpm-list:   
    https://listman.redhat.com/mailman/private/rpm-list/2003-January/017586.html
   and
on aurora-sparc-devel@linuxpower.org:
    http://lists.linuxpower.org/pipermail/aurora-sparc-devel/2003-January/001781.html

---------------------

The first listed script will rebuild about 2/3 of my SRPM
archive of a couple thousand, unattended [takes several days,
btw ...].  Most of the remaining failures are from missing
build dependencies [bith explicit within the .specfile, and
simply functioally needed].  I would like to script having
that script pull and install missing BuildRequires items 
within the .specfile automatically, but have not gotten to it.  

I am working toward that issue on a couple of fronts.  The 
'yum' tool does hands off dependency solving, in a fashion 
which lends itself well to using as the install agent.  

Probably less that 10% of the files out there really _need_ 
manual intervention to get built -- 

My poor little Multia is sitting, patiently, powered off and
next to my SparcStation 2, staring at me with sad eyes, to get
this project advanced, but I had my local SRPM mirror unit 
have a hard drive die last Thursday, and I am not fully back 
online yet.

-- Russ Herrold




