 | On Monday, February 3, 2003, at 01:08  PM, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:
 | > I do have a question... who owns the rights to the EV8?  I know the
 | > chip
 | > will probably never see silicon, but how far along was it in
 | > development?
 | > Wishful thinking I'm sure, but might an interested party build it?
 | 
 | My understanding is that EV8 and all Alpha technology (lock, stock and
 | barrel) was sold to Intel. (Under EV8, Alpha technology was reserved by
 | Compaq, but ?licensed? to Intel -- whatever the lawerish words that
 | allow Samsung to do whatever they can do under their pre-existing
 | contract which covers something -- I don't know if Samsung can produce
 | EV7 or only EV6.)
 | 

from the june 25th, 2002 announcement basically covered that. all technology 
related to the alpha processor and the digital compiler technology was to 
remain compaq technology - it was licenced out to intel for production - 
including the engineers that were working on the ev8 project. the deal also 
detailed that the ev7x team was to be transitioned over after production of the 
ev7x family ceased. it's a similar licensing scheme that was developed with 
oracle for their 9i RAC product (which uses components of the trucluster 
software) but at a much larger scale - including the facilities themselves. 
anyone been to ZKO lately?

in regards to samsung, it's my understanding that they did have access to the 
ev7x technology, but i am uncertain if that's 100% right. in the same merit, 
i'm unsure if amd licensed anything else from the technology as they did with 
the ev6x family. 

Cheers, 

Rodolfo

