All of the software is in the anaconda-runtime package included 
with Red Hat Linux.  Florian laroche has a package on his ftp or 
http space to aide in setting up a build environment.  This is 
the same type of chroot environment that many Red Hatters use on 
their own systems.  I've written my own custom scripts to handle 
it, Florian's are more complete however.

http://people.redhat.com/laroche/rpmutils.tar.gz


> copyrighted,

All software written by anyone, is copyrighted.  The author of a
piece of software automatically is the copyright owner of that
software.  As the owner of the copyright, you decide whom can and
can not use your work, and under what conditions.  This is called
a "license".  The license, is the rights you are granting to
another person, persons, or other group of people potentially
including everyone.

The term "copyright" and "license" are often used 
interchangeably, however they have _very_ different meanings.  
And those meanings also vary around the world.  In Germany for 
example, copyright has 2 meanings, one is more like a 
"birthright" here which you can not ever give up, and the other 
is similar to "copyright" here.


