On Tue, 4 Feb 2003, Mike A. Harris wrote:

> Also, please in the future research these things before making
> false assumptions and/or poor and unfounded accusations and/or
> spreading FUD.

And where did I exactly make unfounded accusations or spead FUD? I said,
"knowing my luck, its " X, Y and Z.

I also said, which the above comment referenced, that I would like to know
how its done at redhat, as I've given some thought lately to totally
rebuilding from source RPM, and a lot of my prior attempts ended in
a good many circular dependencies, and sometimes --force --nodeps helps,
other times, it hurts like hell.

To take a directory full of raw src.rpms and get it to a directory full of
$arch.rpms is a non-trivial task, but made easier if you're running RH7.3
and compiling RH73 src.rpms. To do it across major version numbers is much
more complicated IMNSHO -- so doing something like using a hypothetical
rh7.3 system to make a rh8.0 distro would (just guessing) involve a
chroot set up, compiling and installing intermediate 'bootstrap' rpms into
that chroot (making it a rh73/rh80 mish-mash), then recompiling the
bootstrat rpms in that environment, updating them, and processing the
rest of the src.rpms.

Non-trivial, but RH somehow gets releases out the door.

So, non-trivial, and someone does it, generally better than their
competitors. I would expect parts of that to be 'protected'.

--
