On Fri, 7 Feb 2003, Ted Goodridge wrote:

>That is a pain in the neck for alpha.   There are separate patches you have to 
>apply etc to get it to work on alpha.  Its possible, but definately not easy.
>
>I also like SRPMs because they get you a binary RPM that you can install later 
>:) (If you find your system works, you can use an installer to make a fresh 
>system.)

For do-it-yourselfers, the gotchas are the kernel, glibc, gcc, 
and other core infrastructure components.  Most of the distro is 
basically just a rebuild, with very few Alpha specific changes, 
and many of those aren't even specific to Alpha, but are 64bit or 
similar fixes.  The kernel, glibc, gcc and other major packages 
are where the largest amounts of work need to be done on a new 
distro release for a given architecture.  I don't know the status 
of Alpha running with 8.0ish or later treebase is though.  I've 
been planning on experimenting for ages but haven't had the time.  
;o/

I'm guessing that George France probably knows the current state
better than anyone though, so I'll let him comment if he's 
around...

Take care,
