On Monday 10 February 2003 01:53 am, Mike A. Harris wrote:
> For do-it-yourselfers, the gotchas are the kernel, glibc, gcc,
> and other core infrastructure components.  Most of the distro is
> basically just a rebuild, with very few Alpha specific changes,
> and many of those aren't even specific to Alpha, but are 64bit or
> similar fixes.  The kernel, glibc, gcc and other major packages
> are where the largest amounts of work need to be done on a new
> distro release for a given architecture.  I don't know the status
> of Alpha running with 8.0ish or later treebase is though.  I've
> been planning on experimenting for ages but haven't had the time.
> ;o/
>
> I'm guessing that George France probably knows the current state
> better than anyone though, so I'll let him comment if he's
> around...

As for the kernel, Jeff Wiedemeier and my self have been working on a 
2.4.18-24 update kernel for RH-7.2 for Alpha.  We are attempting to get all 
of the "acceptable" bits in to the 2.4.22 and 2.5.x kernel.org kernels. There 
are still several bits / drivers that will never be in the stock kernel.org 
kernels.  This includes but is not limited to the ferel driver and the 
forward port of 2.4.3 tulip driver.  I would suggest that when 2.4.18-24 for 
RH-7.2 for alpha is available for download, that do-it-yourselfers download 
and install the src rpm and dig through the patches.  All of the patches from 
us should have 'hp' in the file name.

A complete toolchain is made from lots of programs (gcc, binutils, glibc, gdb, 
support tools ( m4, perl, autoconf, automake, bison, flex, autobook, tcl, tk, 
dejagnu, make, gettext, etc..)).  It takes all these applications plus more, 
to make a complete working toolchain.  These programs work together as a 
team.  If one link in the chain is broken, then the entire chain is usually 
broken. For example, If you change the change the version of perl, that you 
use to build autoconf / automake, then you get different gcc testsuite 
results.  Finding the best combinations for an architecture can be very time 
consuming.  As for gcc 3.2.x, it has about twenty-five serious bugs on the 
Alpha Archtecture, that IMHO need to be addressed before I would be happy to 
ship it for commercial use.  Many of  these issues are with the optimizer or 
EV6 / EV67 related.  For the careful do-it-yourselfer, this may not be an 
issue as you could just use -O1 -mcpu=ev5 for building problem applications.

As for the near future, when 2.4.18-24 has been tested and is ready to be made 
available, we will probably create new ISO images with all the current 
updates for RH-7.2 for Alpha, as having people down load the current set of 
ISOs images plus 500+ MBytes of updates does not make much sense (IMHO).

I hope this helps.

Best Regards,



--George






