On Tuesday 11 February 2003 12:44 am, Kelledin wrote:

> Hmmm...interesting.  What exactly goes wrong with openssh?

When compiled with -mcpu=ev6 and -O2 or higher, sshd doesn't accept ssh2 
protocol connections, because a gcc bug makes it drop a block of code that 
sets have_ssh2_key = 1. Oops. There's a workaround patch available, which 
might qualify for Stupidest Patch Ever:

--- sshd.c.orig	2002-11-21 15:42:45.000000000 -0500
+++ sshd.c	2002-11-21 15:43:42.000000000 -0500
@@ -994,6 +994,7 @@
 			sensitive_data.have_ssh1_key = 1;
 			break;
 		case KEY_RSA:
+		        i=i+0; /* gcc3.2 drops these 2 cases without this */
 		case KEY_DSA:
 			sensitive_data.have_ssh2_key = 1;
 			break;

> I ask because I compiled openssh on my LX164 with plain old -O2 a
> while back, and it seems to work OK.  Mind you, I haven't done
> any extensive regression tests on it, so it could have some
> brokenness that I've just dodged through sheer luck.  I also
> haven't done an ev56-optimized compile--it's just straight
> -mcpu=ev4.

If memory serves, -mcpu=ev4 and -mcpu=ev5 generate the same set of 
instructions; the only difference is the scheduling for the different 
processors. So it's fairly useless to optimize for ev4 unless the resulting 
binary will only be run on an ev4.

-will




