On Wednesday 12 February 2003 17:06, John Jasen wrote:
>So far (I admit, advanced workstation src rpms on a 7.3 i386 system) out
>of 690 source rpms, I've had 480 successful compiles, 163 that failed on
>dependencies to build it, and ... 47 failures

Even that won't necessarily give you a suitably stable system, because you've 
probably built the rpms against one set of libraries (e.g. ncurses), then 
built an rpm of a later version of the same. To sort this type of problem out 
I borrowed a technique from the Immunix project (www.immunix.org - it's a 
version of redhat for x86 with security enhancements to gcc and glibc), which 
basically goes.

1) build rpms
2) install rpms
3) build rpms again
4) if new rpm != old rpm, goto (1)

After a few iterations, the system stabilises. But not before you get lost in 
a twisty maze of dependencies, all alike and equally baffling.

cheers
john
 




