On Wed, 12 Feb 2003, dr john halewood wrote:

>Even that won't necessarily give you a suitably stable system, because you've 
>probably built the rpms against one set of libraries (e.g. ncurses), then 
>built an rpm of a later version of the same. To sort this type of problem out 
>I borrowed a technique from the Immunix project (www.immunix.org - it's a 
>version of redhat for x86 with security enhancements to gcc and glibc), which 
>basically goes.
>
>1) build rpms
>2) install rpms
>3) build rpms again
>4) if new rpm != old rpm, goto (1)
>
>After a few iterations, the system stabilises. But not before you get lost in 
>a twisty maze of dependencies, all alike and equally baffling.

That will work with some, but not all software.  Some software 
embeds the date into itself during compilation, or embeds things 
such as the OS kernel used to compile it, the build machine, the 
exact C compiler, etc.

XFree86 for example will not compile 100% identically every time 
it is built.  Functionally it is the same, but the exact binary 
image will not be the same, and so the resulting RPM packages 
wont be the same either.  The kernel does this as well, and 
likely glibc, and other packages also.


-- 
Mike A. Harris     ftp://people.redhat.com/mharris
OS Systems Engineer - XFree86 maintainer - Red Hat




