---------- Forwarded message ----------
Date: Wed, 12 Feb 2003 02:15:47 -0600
From: TapeSupport@seagate.com
To: js290@bellsouth.net
Subject: Re: |Linux|SCSI||My tape drive seems to be

 The drive should be configured as follows:

 Any available SCSI ID 2 through 6 should work; however, we recommend using
 the tape drive on ID 6 if available. Never use ID's 0 or 1; these are
 reserved for boot devices. Configure the tape drive as the last physical
 and terminating device.

 Proper termination is very important when using SCSI devices. Make sure
 both ends of the SCSI bus are properly terminated with Active Termination.
 Consult your SCSI controller documentation for correct termination
 settings.

 The Scorpion 40/240 and Viper drives are not equipped with onboard
 termination.  A cable end terminator or another device must be use to
 provide proper termination.  External models require an Active external
 terminator plug on the tape drive itself or on the last physical device on
 the external BUS.

 It may be necessary to contact the motherboard manufacturer for onboard
 chipsets, the manufacturer of the of the SCSI card for add-in controllers
 or visit their web site to make sure you are using the most current driver
 available for the SCSI controller.


 Using a SCSI tape drive with Windows NT/2000/XP make sure you have the
 latest service pack from Microsoft.

 If you have recently added service pack 4, 5, or 6 to your Windows NT
 system please update your SCSI card drivers.

 Contact the manufacturer of the SCSI card or visit their web site to make
 sure you are using the most current driver available for the SCSI
 controller.

 ************************************************************************************

 In the SCSI BIOS, use the following settings for the SCSI ID of the tape
 drive.  The following settings are for 50pin drives with narrow SCSI.  An
 example would be STT220000N or STD224000N.  Settings for 68pin Wide and
 LVD SCSI are listed below.


 1. Set Sync Negotiation to Yes for DAT drives; No for Travan drives.

 2. Lower the transfer rate as low as the SCSI BIOS allows.

 3. Set Enable Disconnect to Yes.

 4. Set Wide Negotiation to No for Travan and DAT drives.

 5. Send SCSI Start Unit Command to No.

 6. Make sure the Parity setting matches on the controller card and all
 SCSI devices; either all On or all Off.




 Bonita S.
 Seagate Technical Support




