With regards to your message at 11:39 AM 2/13/03, Ted Goodridge, Jr. Where 
you stated:
>Is it possible to get that heatsink off the LX 533 CPU?

There are two aluminium nuts in holes on the top.
The attach it to the CPU package.
Take the whole thing off the boar first.
Unscrew the fan from the heatsink ( 4 Phillips #2 screws)
Use a thin-wall socket to remove the heatsink. I think it is 14mm offhand.
Do NOT touch the graphite heat transfer pad with your fingers.
Use TWEEZERS, and put it in an envelope to protect it.

A copper heat block for water cooling can certainly be made.
Now the tough question:
OK, so you make a heat block, figure out how to make it work with the 
existing heatsink clip, or design and fabricate a new one, use a pump, 
tank, etc.

And the water goes to some form of radiator.

And the radiator is cooled with a fan.

Exactly WHAT have you gained here??

BTW, do NOT think about a peltier.
They suck up enormous amounts of 12V current, and they can chill the CPU 
and socket enough to cause condensation.
Water and electricity do not mix well..
Unless you truly know what you are doing, I do not recommend this route you 
are exploring.



