On Thu, 13 Feb 2003, Ted Goodridge, Jr wrote:

> Is it possible to get that heatsink off the LX 533 CPU?  I know my heatsink
> is very warm (if not hot) to the touch.  If i could get a waterblock to fit
> on it i would be thrilled.  I've been noticing more and more mainstream
> kits hit the market.   I was thinking that if I could get a waterblock on
> it, I would modify a flow regulator/monitor to output to the fan header on
> the motherboard so it would boot, but also shut down if the flow died on
> me.
>
> Yes, I'm slightly insane.  But it never hurts to talk about something
> before you are brave (or dumb, stupid, crazy?) enough to try it.

I was thinking about this awhile back myself, but never really pursued it.
Because my application was going to be for a server, I wanted to rely more
on passive characteristics rather than active ones which could fail
(pumps, fans, etc.); a very large reservior and heat exchanger, convection
flow instead of a pump, and possibly even a flexible braided steel line
(like supply lines for a sink) plumbed into a cold water line.  My hope
was to have a water cooling system that would be extremely resistant to
failure (regardless of cause), and very quiet.  I don't think the
convection flow idea would have worked though, and plumbing a supply line
into the reservior without any kind of automatic shutoff would only have
allowed some bad flooding to occur if there ever was a leak.  The whole
thing ended up getting to be a little much, and more practical things
started taking my attention away.  Fun to think about though.  :-)

I may be wrong, but I doubt you'll have much luck with any existing kits
on the market because of the threaded studs on the cpu.  Ideally you'll
want a waterblock that secures to the cpu in a manner as close as possible
as that of the original heatsink.  You might try asking some of the
outfits that are making waterblocks if they could make you a custom one
taking into account the holes for the studs.

I seem to recall an article I read awhile ago about someone using some
type of flow meter, hooking it to his motherboard's fan header which had
some kind of tach sensor.  I wish I could remember more about it, but you
might have some luck with google searches and/or searching the
overclocking sites.

It should go without saying that you'll need to flow/leak test whatever
system you pull together far from your computer hardware.  Hook everything
together out in the garage, fill the system, fire up the pump and watch
for leaks.  If there are no leaks, then chances are excellent that you'll
never have any leaks when it's installed in your Alpha.  While the water
pressures your system will operate at are actually very low, it might be
worthwhile to pick up an adapter to hook it up to a garden hose, or
something else so you can test it at significantly higher pressure, to
insure against a weak point in the system you hadn't anticipated.

About the only other thing I can think of is put some care into the
routing and positioning of the water lines in the Alpha's case.  Water
lines rubbing against other components over time, or touching components
hot enough to melt a weak spot in the tubing would be bad things, and yet
easy to deal with in advance.

If you do make this work, I would absolutely love to hear about it.  Good
luck,
Mike
