I have a 433au also. So I can say definitively that there is no halt
switch (that I have ever been able to locate). You need to enter SRM,
which is what it has to be using to boot OSF. You do that, as inidicated
below, by pressing ctrl-c during POST like a rabid animal. If it doesn't
work, use the reset swtich and keep going until you get to the SRM prompt.
>From there you can use the 'show dev' command to get a device list. And
use the 'boot <dev>'  to boot from that device.

>From there follow all of the other good advice about restoring your shells
or install RedHat (or other linux distro).

Thanks,

JAmes


>
> (Until somebody replies who _really_ knows what's going on...)
>
> If the shells are gone, that's pretty bad.
>
> To install Redhat, you first need to get to the firmware
> that runs the bootloader that reads the kernel that brings
> up the OS.  If you're lucky, and this is likely, the machine
> uses SRM firmware.  When resetting or powering up, you should
> see some blue background white foreground text as it does POST
> and boots the OS.  You need to get a ">>>" prompt rather than
> the OS boot sequence.  One way is to press ^C a whole bunch of
> times during the POST and related stuff.  Many machines have
> jumpers or a "halt" switch that will give the SRM prompt rather
> than booting the OS.  Until somebody who knows the 433au
> replies, you might find documentation on the web.  The mailing
> list archives at redhat.com will likely show answers to similar
> questions/issues.
>
> Once you get the ">>>" prompt, do "show dev" to see what disks
> are visible to SRM.  With luck, the CDROM drive will show up as
> DQA0 or DKA400 or something.  With more luck, "boot DKA..."
> (with '...' replaced by the rest of the actual device name) will
> bring up the installer.
>
> With luck, somebody with much better knowledge about the 433au
> will reply soon.
>
> Good luck.
