That actually sounds like a pretty interesting project.  Since 
I'm unemployed and have lots of time on my hands, I might 
possibly pitch in here.  There's a few things I'd need though...

1) I'd probably have to learn assembly language for Alpha 
processors.  Any good documentation on this?

2) I'd have to learn exactly what goes on with the Alpha system 
from power on at least up until the O/S load.  I'm pretty sure 
the initial part works like x86--the system boots, and the CPU 
spins up with the instruction pointer pointing to a certain 
address in memory corresponding to the beginning of AlphaBIOS or 
SRM bootstrap code.  Eventually that brings the system to the 
point that it can execute a bootloader from disk, and then 
things are largely out of our hands.

3) I'd have to have a good source of Alpha hardware, or at least 
have a failsafe means to repair the firmware on my LX164 if I 
destroy it while working on this stuff.  Being unemployed means 
I'm also poor; I was lucky to even be able to afford an LX164 in 
the first place.

A first step for me might be to get enough bootstrap code 
together for my 2940UW to boot and be able to run its EPROM 
setup on my LX164.  If I learn how and don't wreck anything, I 
could do that without any extra hardware donations.  Emulating 
up to a 486 wouldn't be such a problem, given time; I still have 
a 486 manual around here that details all the opcodes known for 
that architecture.  Later x86 arches would be rather more of a 
PITA...

-- 
Kelledin
