I know this isn't exactly on the same topic.  I just remembered a 
packaging system that I saw mentioned on /. a few months ago.  I think 
it was put together by mostly a French developer's team.  It was sort of 
like the BSD ports tool.  A package description would fetch the source 
and could be setup to also grab and apply patches.  The description file 
then had defaults for the configuration of the package.  It was 
hierarchy based in the file system.  You could override defaults by 
editing files higher up in the hierarchy.

I think some small distro was using it for it's packages, but other 
developers had stepped in to add other packages.  It seemed an ideal 
cross between Gentoo and Linux From Scratch.  It gave the person putting 
together the system a little more control than Gentoo, but not the huge 
amount of effort required by LFS.

I wish I could remember what this fetch/configure/build system was 
called.  I'm sure there aren't that many predefined packages 
specifically for the Alpha.  But I do everything the LFS way right now, 
and am just looking for a tool to help my memory as to what options I 
pass to ./configure and to make sure packages get build with the same 
compiler flags.

-- 
Chris




