Yeah, I have three 2100A's that I would really like to dedicate to a
compute cluster under Linux.
I wonder why those responsible for porting Linux to alpha way back when
only chose to support 2100's, when the 2100A is more cutting edge with
the dual PCI buses?
 
Don't get me wrong.  I think the folks at DEC/Compaq/HP (and others) who
are responsible for the Alpha port have done a fabulous job, and I
understand that the PCI interrupt architecture is a little strange on
the 2100A, but it runs DUNIX so well.
 
Not to mention that FreeBSD supports the 2100A (lousy on smp though).
 
In fact, on large jobs a two-node quad 300 MHz 2100A cluster outperforms
my Itanium2 box due to the increased CPU count and cache bandwidth.
 
 
 
 
Michael A. Mackey (michael-mackey@uiowa.edu)
Associate Professor
Biomedical Engineering / Pathology
University of Iowa, Iowa City, Iowa
Voice: 319-335-6058
FAX:  319-335-5631
