On Wed, Feb 26, 2003 at 05:18:37PM -0600, Michael A. Mackey wrote:
>
> I wonder why those responsible for porting Linux to alpha way back when
> only chose to support 2100's, when the 2100A is more cutting edge with
> the dual PCI buses?

Because "those responsible" never even saw one of those puppies until
someone was purging their lab and needed the space that it was taking
up... :-\

Not to mention the lack of available internal documentation on the one
critical area where the 2100A varied from the 2100 - interrupt
management hardware. Thanks to the efforts of Jim Gettys (IIRC), the
pertinent Tru64 code and docs were released to the *BSD folks, hence
their port. Someone in that camp obviously had a box to work with,
which always makes things easier... ;-}

> Don't get me wrong.  I think the folks at DEC/Compaq/HP (and others) who
> are responsible for the Alpha port have done a fabulous job, and I
> understand that the PCI interrupt architecture is a little strange on
> the 2100A, but it runs DUNIX so well.

Yup, given EV5 CPUs and enough memory...

> Not to mention that FreeBSD supports the 2100A (lousy on smp though).

Yeah, but can they run on a WILDFIRE or a MARVEL? :-) :-)

 --Jay++

